---
description: Core constraints for the goodreads-mcp read-only MCP server
alwaysApply: true
---

Full guidance: [AGENTS.md](mdc:AGENTS.md). Essentials:

- Read-only MCP server over public Goodreads data. No auth, no cookies, no
  writes. Write operations are out of scope.
- No public Goodreads API exists. Data comes from shelf RSS, the JSON
  autocomplete endpoint, `__NEXT_DATA__` on Next.js pages, AppSync GraphQL,
  and a best-effort HTML scrape used only by `list_shelves`.
- On Next.js book pages, parse `__NEXT_DATA__` / Apollo state, not the markup.
  `list_shelves` is the deliberate exception (shelf names have no structured
  surface); don't extend scraping beyond it.
- Book HTML pages sit behind an AWS WAF JS challenge (HTTP 202) — fetch them
  via the `.xml`-suffixed path.
- Never hardcode the GraphQL key or endpoint; `client.graphql_config` resolves
  both at runtime so rotations self-heal.
- Preserve the single shared client, backoff on 429/503, and browser-faithful
  headers. Don't add concurrency that raises request rate.
- New discovery tools: reuse `_paginated_graphql_edges`, cap `limit` at 100,
  return `returned` / `has_more`, and include source `url` fields.
- `pytest -q` is offline. `GOODREADS_LIVE=1 pytest tests/e2e -v` is live — run
  it after any parsing change.
